dbHarness proposes to establish a secure, extensible and cost-efficient internal AI agent platform powered by open-weight models deployed on Deutsche Bank’s existing GPU infrastructure.

The initiative aims to explore whether internally hosted AI models, combined with a customizable open-source agent harness such as Pi, can provide a technically competitive and economically scalable alternative to commercial AI coding-agent solutions.

The initial proof of concept will focus on an end-to-end software engineering workflow: investigating defects in complex codebases, identifying root causes, implementing fixes, preparing test data, executing validation in isolated environments, and generating draft pull requests for human review.

The proposed solution will be benchmarked against existing approved capabilities, including Gemini CLI, with emphasis on successful task completion, reliability, developer intervention, execution time, and total cost per validated outcome.

The initiative will begin with limited infrastructure allocation and a controlled engineering beta, working closely with dbLLM to evaluate suitable open-weight models. If successful, dbHarness can evolve into a reusable agent execution and model-consumption platform, integrated with existing Ada capabilities to simplify access to internally hosted models.

Over time, this collaborative foundation could enable developers and applications to consume coding, reasoning, OCR, embedding, vision, and other specialized AI models through approved interfaces and extensible workflows.

The long-term objective is to accelerate enterprise adoption of open-weight AI, increase the practical value derived from existing GPU investments, and establish an evidence-based route toward scalable, governed, and economically sustainable internal AI capabilities.



